academic research and open-source software libraries exist explicitly dedicated to connecting multiple clothoids. [1] (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/273575967_Design_considerations_for_highway_reverse_curves), [2] (https://github.com/ebertolazzi/G1fitting/blob/master/README.md)

In civil engineering, racetrack layouts, and robotics path planning, a line that transitions seamlessly to return in the opposite direction is known as an S-Shape Pair or a Reverse Curve. 
[1] (https://support.midasuser.com/hc/en-us/articles/35984748482969-Clothoid), 
[2] (https://onlinepubs.trb.org/Onlinepubs/trr/1994/1445/1445-001.pdf), [3] (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/273575967_Design_considerations_for_highway_reverse_curves)

Because a standard clothoid naturally curves in only one direction, reversing the track's trajectory requires a specialized mathematical handshake. 
[1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfNijLrIcTs)

1. The Math of a Reverse Clothoid Curve

A single clothoid has a single, constant sign for its curvature. To make a track snake in the opposite direction, you must connect a positive curvature clothoid (turning left) to a negative curvature clothoid (turning right). [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfNijLrIcTs)

The point where they connect is called the Inflection Point. At this exact junction, the track must satisfy two critical design rules: [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfNijLrIcTs)
1. Curvature drops to Zero (\(\kappa = 0\)): The steering wheel must be perfectly straight at the moment of reversal.
2. \(G^{2}\) Geometric Continuity: The transition must be seamless. You cannot jump directly from a left-hand curve to a right-hand curve without a flat interface, otherwise, vehicles would instantly spin out or break. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EMeGqOE2QQ4), [2] (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/300888170_Better_Road_Design_Using_Clothoids), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s9LWqewG8EY&t=476), [4] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfNijLrIcTs)
