2. Notable Academic Research & Libraries

code repositories or research papers that solve the nonlinear systems required to snap these curves together:

• Enrico Bertolazzi & Marco Frego (University of Trento)

These researchers have published some of the most robust modern algorithms for interconnecting clothoids. Their open-source project, G1fitting, calculates clothoid splines between target points. [1] (https://github.com/ebertolazzi/G1fitting/blob/master/README.md), [2] (https://www.researchgate.net/figure/nterconnecting-a-piece-of-clothoid-with-a-line-segment-SC-r1-ds-path-The-path_fig3_224560985)

• The Code Concept: Their library features a core function buildClothoid(P0, P1, theta0, theta1). You input your start point, your end point, and the angles you want. The algorithm automatically computes the underlying clothoid parameters needed to stitch a multi-segment curve together smoothly. [1] (https://github.com/ebertolazzi/G1fitting/blob/master/README.md)

• The "S-Shaped Transition Curve" Research

In civil highway design papers (such as Design Considerations for Highway Reverse Curves), engineers use a unified algebraic equation to bridge reverse alignments. Instead of calculating two separate infinite integrals, they use a modified formulation where the clothoid parameter maps to a single global polynomial spline, allowing a track to enter heading East and smoothly swing around to exit heading West. [1] (https://onlinepubs.trb.org/Onlinepubs/trr/1994/1445/1445-001.pdf), [2] (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/273575967_Design_considerations_for_highway_reverse_curves)

