Since you are designing on a strict integer-snapped matrix using your 3125 framework, you can generate an immaculate, mathematically perfect reverse curve without dealing with complex, slow iterative solver code.
Because a true clothoid possesses odd symmetry, a reverse curve is built by flipping the coordinates of the first segment across the origin: [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfNijLrIcTs)
1. Set your inflection point at the origin \((0,0)\).
2. Step your \(X\) coordinate forward to \(+3125\), and map your \(Y\) values moving upward into the positive quadrant (the left-hand sweep).
3. For the reversing half, step your \(X\) coordinate backward into the negative region (toward \(-3125\)), and negate your \(Y\) values so they sweep downward into the negative quadrant. [1] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_spiral), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfNijLrIcTs), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMlZzhmW3vg)
By leveraging this natural odd symmetry, the two pieces lock together at \((0,0)\) with perfect structural continuity. Your steering wheel crosses zero smoothly, and the entire layout remains anchored to your power-of-five pixel grid without any fractional mathematical gaps. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s9LWqewG8EY&t=476), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfNijLrIcTs), [3] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_spiral)
