No. Not one line of geometry is being ported to Python. What I added is ~200 lines of C++ wrapper in csrc/ plus ctypes bindings; the engine stays exactly where it is. The drafted batch is C++ too.
The asymmetry that decides it
Layer	Lines	Owner	Risk if we fork it
Clothoids + submodules	~103,000 C++	Bertolazzi/Frego, BSD-2	Fresnel, solvers, Eigen, AABB, intersections. Years to re-derive, easy to get subtly wrong.
Our C surface	~340 C++	us, MIT	Small, testable, the actual contract.
Python package	~3,100	us, MIT	Policy, IO, layout, CLI. Cheap to change.
Porting 100k lines of numerical C++ to Python buys nothing and costs the thing that matters: the invariant surface you have to defend. The library is the reference implementation; our wrapper is the part that can actually be wrong, and it is small enough to test exhaustively. That is the whole architecture: rent the mathematics, own the contract.
Where the boundary is, and what crosses it
Plain C, not C++ types: double arrays in, error codes out, one opaque handle per chain. No exceptions across the ABI, no callbacks, no STL types. That is why ctypes works with zero runtime dependencies — which matters on an 8 GB machine next to Inkscape.
The cost of ctypes is that signatures are declared twice, once in C and once in Python, and nothing checks them until runtime. You saw the failure mode: symbol not found after a stale dylib. Senior fix is not pybind11 by reflex; it is:
- an ABI version exported by the library and checked at load;
- a build stamp (git hash + vendor commit) in --version;
- generated bindings, or one table, once the surface passes ~30 functions. At 29 it is already at the edge.
pybind11/nanobind become worth it when you need C++ types, callbacks, or many overloads. We need none of that. If we ever do, that is a boundary redesign, not a port.
The performance number that matters
Measured on this machine:
Python -> C++ call        3.6 µs
chain build, 64 segments  0.3 ms
render  3 segments        114 ms
render 12 segments        396 ms
render 32 segments      1,029 ms
render 64 segments      2,059 ms
The engine is not the slow part. The render is ~32 ms per segment, and it is Python-side error search calling point() hundreds of thousands of times across the boundary. That is an architectural smell, and the fix is not a rewrite:
- use the library's own projection (findST1 / closest_point_ISO) instead of a Python arc-length search;
- batch evaluations in C for the hot loops;
- set a budget — 12 segments under 100 ms — and benchmark it in CI.
This is the senior distinction: the language is rarely the problem; the call pattern is. A 3 µs boundary is fine at 1,000 calls and fatal at 800,000.
Maturity gaps I would fix before more features
1. ABI version + build stamp. A Python package that assumes symbols exist will fail cryptically. Version the C surface, refuse to load on mismatch.
2. Header dependency tracking. The build cache checks object existence only; edit a vendor header and the dylib is silently stale. Content-keyed cache, or let the compiler emit .d files.
3. Upstream sync policy. Vendored at a pinned commit. Decide: submodule + patch queue, or subtree. Either way, document how to move the pin and re-run the numerical tests.
4. Backend policy. Two backends doubles the test matrix for little product value. The wheel is a legitimate oracle for the wrapper; it is not a second product. Make that explicit, or delete it.
5. Distribution. A wheel per platform, or build-from-source; universal2, rpath, codesign/notarize on macOS, .so on Linux. "pip install then nothing works" is the current story.
6. Test pyramid. The numerical invariants are good. Add: property tests (symmetry, closure), golden SVGs, fuzz on JSON/points, an ABI smoke test, and one benchmark.
What I would rewrite
Nothing in the engine. Two things I might reimplement, deliberately:
- G1 tangent estimation for point splines. The library uses a local biarc guess; the paper's global curvature-variation method is a different algorithm, not a translation. That is an algorithm upgrade, justified by shape quality, not by language.
- The width envelope for terminals. No library has it; it is new mathematics, and Python is the right place to prototype it before it earns C++.
Everything else — model, composition, checks, layout, the Inkscape/desktop bridge — belongs in Python and should stay there. Rust or JS here would add toolchains and remove nothing.
The one-sentence version: we are not porting C to Python; we are trying to stop paying 800,000 boundary crossings for an error metric the C++ can answer directly.


