what do you mean  press 5 once and save in Inkscape;?
+Thought · 11.0s
Two separate actions, and neither is required if the view is already right:
1. Press 5 — that's Inkscape's built-in shortcut for View → Zoom → Page ("fit page in window"), from its default keymap (win.canvas-zoom-page). It fits the whole 200 × 250 sheet in the pane. No custom key needed.
2. Save (Cmd+S) — Inkscape writes the current zoom and pan into the file's <sodipodi:namedview>. That's the step that makes the view stick. Without saving, 5 only changes your current session; the file still contains whatever view it had before.
Why I suggested it: fit_view.py computes the zoom from the page size and your window size, which is a calculation and can be off by a little. Inkscape itself knows the true value. If you open the file, press 5, and save, the file then contains Inkscape's own exact view — no guessing. The watcher preserves that namedview on every rebuild, so it stays fixed.
Caveat: saving makes Inkscape rewrite the SVG in its own format (it adds sodipodi/inkscape attributes and may reformat the path data). The next watcher rebuild replaces the drawing anyway and keeps only the view, so this is harmless — but save as Inkscape SVG, not "Plain SVG", or the namedview will be dropped.
If the file now opens fitted at 74%, you don't need either step — just use it. The 5 + save route is the fallback if 74% turns out wrong on your screen.


